    notes: 'The literature is remarkably consistent in quantifying the cost of manual scheduling. Chen et al. in 2021 surveyed university course timetabling across multiple institutions and documented that sixty-two percent of universities still use manual or semi-automated scheduling, with consistently elevated scheduling conflict rates, longer administrative resolution cycles, and lower faculty and student satisfaction compared to institutions using algorithmic tools. Bui et al. in 2022 investigated metaheuristic approaches to academic timetabling and found that algorithmic scheduling reduced the planning cycle from twenty-one days to three days while achieving complete hard-constraint satisfaction — no room double-bookings, no faculty conflicts, no student section overlaps. That is an eighty-six percent reduction in administrative time. Veenstra and Vis in 2023 documented that manual schedule conflict density averages eight to twelve percent — meaning eight to twelve percent of scheduled assignments contain a conflict requiring manual correction. Diallo et al. in 2024 demonstrated that constraint satisfaction-based systems can achieve one hundred percent hard-constraint satisfaction with seventy-eight percent soft-constraint fulfillment. These statistics do not merely justify SEN-GEN\'s proposed scheduling engine — they make it an operational necessity for an institution currently spending eighteen to twenty-two working days on a task that algorithmic tools can complete in two to four.',
