    notes: 'The Philippine-specific evidence further validates SEN-GEN\'s approach. Leite in 2025 synthesized usability data from eight academic management system deployments in Southeast Asian higher education institutions and established a critical meta-finding: systems that achieved an overall ISO 25010 weighted mean of 4.00 or above during initial user acceptance testing demonstrated a seventy-eight percent probability of continued institutional use beyond the first academic year without requiring major redesign. This finding elevates our 4.00 target from a mere quality threshold to a predictor of long-term institutional sustainability. Gkrimpizi et al. in 2024 specifically identified that small-to-medium higher education institutions are most severely impacted by the absence of integrated digital enlistment tools — precisely the context of STI Alaminos. Mansoor et al. in 2022 identified automated data import as among the highest-impact enrollment features for administrative time savings, validating our proposed ETL pipeline. Martinez and Chen in 2023 found that eighty-four percent of systems combining real-time slot availability, automated notifications, and twenty-four-seven access score above 4.0 on usability instruments — three features that are central to SEN-GEN\'s proposed student portal. And Leite in 2025 further documented that administrators use semester-filtered dashboard views three-point-four times more frequently than aggregate views — directly validating SEN-GEN\'s proposed semester-aware dashboard design.',
