    notes: 'Beyond the scheduling engine, the literature validates each of SEN-GEN\'s other five proposed modules. For the ETL pipeline — Extract, Transform, Load — Mansoor et al. in 2022 documented that automated XLSX import is among the highest-impact enrollment features for administrative efficiency, directly reducing data entry errors and accelerating pre-enlistment authorization. For Role-Based Access Control, Liu et al. in 2017 examined RBAC architectures in educational administration systems and demonstrated that a tiered role structure — distinguishing student, faculty, administrative, and supervisory access levels — is operationally sound and corresponds directly to SEN-GEN\'s proposed six-role hierarchy. Kim and Park in 2022 further confirmed that RBAC significantly reduces unauthorized access risks in institutional information systems. For the semester-aware dashboard, Leite in 2025 documented that administrators use semester-filtered views three-point-four times more than aggregate historical views, and that the most valued features are enrollment count by section, room utilization percentage, and faculty load distribution — all of which are planned for SEN-GEN\'s dashboard. For automated notifications, Martinez and Chen in 2023 found that platforms incorporating five or more automated notification events achieve student-reported satisfaction scores forty-one percent higher than platforms without. SEN-GEN will trigger six notification events across the enrollment lifecycle. And for the student enlistment portal, Bellio et al. in 2022 established that pre-publication of complete, constraint-verified schedules substantially reduces manual administrative transaction volume during peak enlistment periods.',
